# Build context exclusions.
#
# The rule applied here: anything the fast suite READS stays, everything else
# goes. That is not the same as "anything large goes" -- see the notes at the
# bottom, all of which cost image size to keep the suite honest.
#
# Before excluding anything, run the suite in the image. Two of the entries that
# looked obviously safe were not, and neither failure was visible from reading
# the tests: they build their paths as `REPO_ROOT / "analysis" / "results"`, so
# grep finds nothing. tests/test_packaging_metadata.py now derives the protected
# set from the AST for exactly that reason.

**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
.mypy_cache
*.egg-info
build/
dist/

# Rendered figures. Regenerated by analysis/*.py and validation/figures/*.py;
# no test reads the committed images.
analysis/figures
validation/figures

# Manuscript sources. scripts/make_paper_figures.py WRITES paper/figures (it is
# that script's default output directory, created on demand) and
# tests/test_make_paper_figures.py exercises the generator against tmp_path, so
# nothing reads the committed copies.
paper

# Notebooks. Exploratory, not part of any checked invariant.
**/*.ipynb
.ipynb_checkpoints

# Training artifacts and generated datasets (also in .gitignore, listed again
# because a developer's working tree usually has them).
runs/
data/synthetic/

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
.vscode
.idea
*.swp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dockerfile, .dockerignore and .github/ -- a few KB, and the fast suite reads
# all three. tests/test_packaging_metadata.py asserts that the image installs
# from the hash-pinned lock, that this file does not exclude anything the suite
# needs, and that the workflow has its four jobs. Excluding them (the
# conventional choice) makes `docker run --rm sc` fail three tests with
# FileNotFoundError, which is a worse outcome than a marginally larger context.
# Shipping them also makes the image self-describing: `docker run --rm sc cat
# Dockerfile` shows how it was built.
#
# validation/results/ (~26 MB) -- tests/test_validation_freeze.py hash-pins
# every artifact under it against FROZEN_MANIFEST.md, in the DEFAULT suite.
# Excluding it turns the freeze guard from a check into a no-op, which is the
# single worst thing this file could do.
#
# analysis/results/ (~5 MB) -- looks regenerable, is not. Five test modules read
# it: test_provenance.py:43, test_regression_figures.py:60,
# test_noise_metrology.py:369, test_soliton_staircase.py:45 and
# test_noise_validation_campaign.py:38. Excluding it was tried and produced 3
# failures and 3 errors inside the image. Note that grepping the tests for the
# string "analysis/results" finds NOTHING -- every one of them builds the path
# as `REPO_ROOT / "analysis" / "results"`, which is why
# tests/test_packaging_metadata.py derives the protected set from the AST
# instead of from a text search.
#
# .git/ (~48 MB) -- the expensive one, and the one whose absence is hardest to
# diagnose. simulator/provenance.py:70 stamps artifacts by running
# `git -C <repo> rev-parse`; with no .git it degrades to "unknown" rather than
# raising, so the image builds and runs and every artifact it produces is
# silently untraceable. tests/test_provenance.py catches it only because it
# asserts a 40-character hash. Keeping .git is also the point of a
# reproducibility image: an artifact that cannot name the commit that made it is
# not reproducible. (The `git` binary is installed in the Dockerfile for the
# same reason -- python:3.11-slim has neither.)
#
# tests/data/ (~13 MB, of which golden/ is ~12.5 MB) -- kept ENTIRE. The
# obvious-looking rule "drop tests/data but keep golden/" inverts the actual
# dependency: golden/ is the bulk, while the loose fixtures it would discard are
# ~45 KB and are loaded live by tests/test_dks_labeling.py:70 and
# tests/test_dispersion_grid.py:176. Dropping them costs nothing measurable and
# breaks the suite, so the whole directory ships.
